From 201e909e918452b4ccf8453cb7e1b21b8dbe7e12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 16:51:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Smooth Motion: the Mac's water and HUD (indexed meshes
 blended vertex by vertex with their UVs, screen sprites matched by bounds)

From Wind Waker Recomp's patches/recompcore/0170 (the Mac line's 0113 and
0160; its lava part, 0120, is this fork's 81d7345 and 7c62903 already):

- An indexed mesh the game deforms on the CPU (the sea's grid, which
  follows the player) is keyed by its material and index stream and has
  each vertex blended, and its direct UVs with them, so the sea's texture no
  longer slides in 30 Hz steps under smooth waves. Such draws keep their own
  vertices (not batched) while frames are interpolated.
- A depth-less 2D sprite drawn with direct positions is matched by its
  transformed bounds (the nearest unused rectangle of its texture and tint)
  rather than by its place among copies of one texture, so counters and
  hearts do not borrow each other's motion.
- Untagged positions use an absolute tolerance, so world coordinates of
  hundreds of thousands of units still show their motion.

Merged with this fork's cloth strips (a scope over indexed draws keeps its
scope key and is blended as a tagged particle) and blended colours (the
per-step pixel blocks); frame_interp_test passes with both sets of cases.
